    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-1",
      "sourceStart": 403,
      "sourceEnd": 420,
      "original": "## 1. Definitions",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0002",
      "sourceStart": 420,
      "sourceEnd": 422,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-1-1",
      "sourceStart": 422,
      "sourceEnd": 450,
      "original": "### 1.1. Invariant Subspaces",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0003",
      "sourceStart": 450,
      "sourceEnd": 452,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-1-1",
      "sourceStart": 452,
      "sourceEnd": 533,
      "original": "#### 1.1.1. A linear map from a vector space to itself is called an **operator**.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0004",
      "sourceStart": 533,
      "sourceEnd": 535,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-1-2",
      "sourceStart": 535,
      "sourceEnd": 669,
      "original": "#### 1.1.2. Suppose $T \\in \\mathcal{L}(V)$. A subspace $U$ of $V$ is called **invariant** under $T$ if $Tu \\in U$ for every $u \\in U$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0005",
      "sourceStart": 669,
      "sourceEnd": 742,
      "sha256": "0019712217ca1f506e9efe035a157d4285c449d4d14e897ebe450c9fd9c5aa7f"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-1-3",
      "sourceStart": 742,
      "sourceEnd": 911,
      "original": "#### 1.1.3. Suppose $T \\in \\mathcal{L}(V)$. A number $\\lambda \\in F$ is called an **eigenvalue** of $T$ if there exists $v\\in V$ such that $v \\neq 0$ and $Tv=\\lambda v$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0006",
      "sourceStart": 911,
      "sourceEnd": 913,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-1-4",
      "sourceStart": 913,
      "sourceEnd": 1114,
      "original": "#### 1.1.4. Suppose $T \\in \\mathcal{L}(V)$ and $\\lambda\\in F$ is an eigenvalue of $T$. A vector $v\\in V$ is called an **eigenvector** of $T$ corresponding to $\\lambda$ if $v \\neq 0$ and $Tv=\\lambda v$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0007",
      "sourceStart": 1114,
      "sourceEnd": 1116,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-1-5",
      "sourceStart": 1116,
      "sourceEnd": 1209,
      "original": "#### 1.1.5. Suppose $T \\in \\mathcal{L}(V)$ and $p\\in \\mathcal{P}(F)$ is a polynomial given by",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0008",
      "sourceStart": 1209,
      "sourceEnd": 1534,
      "sha256": "e931611e0f6516fe29851a3be577dbb7a81713f9bfda76c590442d5c9eb2942b"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-1-6",
      "sourceStart": 1534,
      "sourceEnd": 1722,
      "original": "#### 1.1.6. Suppose $V$ is finite-dimensional, $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$, and $U$ is a subspace of $V$ invariant under $T$. Then the **quotient operator** $T/U\\in\\mathcal{L}(V/U)$ is defined by",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0009",
      "sourceStart": 1722,
      "sourceEnd": 1852,
      "sha256": "aa098a6d34ff6058c51c6c8b05d9b8f4df65b0542ed1ae675cc4cf26d261285d"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-1-2",
      "sourceStart": 1852,
      "sourceEnd": 1883,
      "original": "### 1.2. The Minimal Polynomial",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0010",
      "sourceStart": 1883,
      "sourceEnd": 1885,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-2-1",
      "sourceStart": 1885,
      "sourceEnd": 2082,
      "original": "#### 1.2.1. Suppose $V$ is finite-dimensional and $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$. Then the minimal polynomial of $T$ is the unique monic polynomial $p\\in\\mathcal{P}(F)$ of smallest degree such that $p(T)=0$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0011",
      "sourceStart": 2082,
      "sourceEnd": 2084,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-3",
      "sourceStart": 2084,
      "sourceEnd": 2118,
      "original": "### 1.3. Upper-Triangular Matrices",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0012",
      "sourceStart": 2118,
      "sourceEnd": 2192,
      "sha256": "51e55f9c7525fa1c9088440331e32d3f5c651cc71b330957cf1a0bbcd6667a16"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-1-4",
      "sourceStart": 2192,
      "sourceEnd": 2225,
      "original": "### 1.4. Diagonalizable Operators",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0013",
      "sourceStart": 2225,
      "sourceEnd": 2227,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-4-1",
      "sourceStart": 2227,
      "sourceEnd": 2405,
      "original": "#### 1.4.1. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space. An operator on $V$ is called diagonalizable if the operator has a diagonal matrix with respect to some basis of $V$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0014",
      "sourceStart": 2405,
      "sourceEnd": 2407,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-4-2",
      "sourceStart": 2407,
      "sourceEnd": 2565,
      "original": "#### 1.4.2. Suppose $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$ and $\\lambda\\in F$. The eigenspace of $T$ corresponding to $\\lambda$ is the subspace $E(\\lambda,T)$ of $V$ defined by",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0015",
      "sourceStart": 2565,
      "sourceEnd": 2631,
      "sha256": "e6a0b34a905d20b70a5a3d05460142d62f13f93802985b61a306866f8d94240d"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-4-3",
      "sourceStart": 2631,
      "sourceEnd": 2892,
      "original": "#### 1.4.3. <span style=\"color: red\">(Gershgorin disks)</span> Suppose $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$, $\\mathscr{B}=\\{v_1,\\ldots,v_n\\}$ is a basis of $V$, and $A=[T]_{\\mathscr{B}}$. A **Gershgorin disk** of $T$ with respect to the basis $\\mathscr{B}$ is a set of the form",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0016",
      "sourceStart": 2892,
      "sourceEnd": 3016,
      "sha256": "35fed27b8e62f9d5c473725b36579b911bedc254a6a4212f08a637bcfb71b54b"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-1-5",
      "sourceStart": 3016,
      "sourceEnd": 3044,
      "original": "### 1.5. Commuting Operators",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0017",
      "sourceStart": 3044,
      "sourceEnd": 3046,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-1-5-1",
      "sourceStart": 3046,
      "sourceEnd": 3138,
      "original": "#### 1.5.1. Two operators $S,T$ on the same vector space are said to **commute** if $ST=TS$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0018",
      "sourceStart": 3138,
      "sourceEnd": 3267,
      "sha256": "b58c59ee1c97297739e3c8b38e5ca3ed8be9cbd066be99030e786ca30c07b073"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-2",
      "sourceStart": 3267,
      "sourceEnd": 3281,
      "original": "## 2. Theorems",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0019",
      "sourceStart": 3281,
      "sourceEnd": 3283,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-2-1",
      "sourceStart": 3283,
      "sourceEnd": 3311,
      "original": "### 2.1. Invariant Subspaces",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0020",
      "sourceStart": 3311,
      "sourceEnd": 3313,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-1-1",
      "sourceStart": 3313,
      "sourceEnd": 3449,
      "original": "#### 2.1.1. Suppose $V$ is finite-dimensional, $T\\in \\mathcal{L}(V)$, and $\\lambda \\in F$. Then the following statements are equivalent.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0021",
      "sourceStart": 3449,
      "sourceEnd": 3604,
      "sha256": "5dcd552491ab0c99fb63928ca34f99569ea73ae944352e14ae7b9ac57aa9addf"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-1-2",
      "sourceStart": 3604,
      "sourceEnd": 3754,
      "original": "#### 2.1.2. Suppose $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$. Then every list of eigenvectors of $T$ corresponding to distinct eigenvalues of $T$ is linearly independent.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0022",
      "sourceStart": 3754,
      "sourceEnd": 4096,
      "sha256": "a4740e58e189cf9b40bfcd2c346f09552704ae557bdb8a2c416b2370256ba5c6"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-1-3",
      "sourceStart": 4096,
      "sourceEnd": 4238,
      "original": "#### 2.1.3. Suppose $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$ and $p\\in\\mathcal{P}(F)$. Then $\\ker(p(T))$ and $\\operatorname{range}(p(T))$ are invariant under $T$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0023",
      "sourceStart": 4238,
      "sourceEnd": 4240,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-1-4",
      "sourceStart": 4240,
      "sourceEnd": 4431,
      "original": "#### 2.1.4. Suppose $V$ is finite-dimensional, $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V),\\ \\lambda\\in F$. Then $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $T$ if and only if $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of the dual operator $T'$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0024",
      "sourceStart": 4431,
      "sourceEnd": 4604,
      "sha256": "1de301b3ce15e7ad249cbecc5c0ae7852fc249da5f47af71f8358a7af63856ff"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-1-5",
      "sourceStart": 4604,
      "sourceEnd": 4708,
      "original": "#### 2.1.5. Let $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$ and let $m\\geq1$ be an integer. Then the following statements hold.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0025",
      "sourceStart": 4708,
      "sourceEnd": 4810,
      "sha256": "206aeb071959bc5fc86f632f61e09e7bed30c99cc532229ff140172b1401d89a"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-1-6",
      "sourceStart": 4810,
      "sourceEnd": 5031,
      "original": "#### 2.1.6. Suppose $V$ is finite-dimensional. Then $\\beta = \\{v_1,\\ldots,v_m\\}$ is linearly independent if and only if there exists $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$ such that $\\beta$ is a set of eigenvectors of distinct eigenvalues.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0026",
      "sourceStart": 5031,
      "sourceEnd": 5350,
      "sha256": "db7d216cffc71c465e8af975debd4648e3a8b1c04bbce77d3076357f544fd9c0"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-2-2",
      "sourceStart": 5350,
      "sourceEnd": 5381,
      "original": "### 2.2. The Minimal Polynomial",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0027",
      "sourceStart": 5381,
      "sourceEnd": 5383,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-2-1",
      "sourceStart": 5383,
      "sourceEnd": 5481,
      "original": "#### 2.2.1. Every operator on a finite-dimensional nonzero complex vector space has an eigenvalue.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0028",
      "sourceStart": 5481,
      "sourceEnd": 5750,
      "sha256": "87d819458c588f89f1503d9405921efed0307af531f3c92f085b72f739770842"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-2-2",
      "sourceStart": 5750,
      "sourceEnd": 5973,
      "original": "#### 2.2.2. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space and $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$. Then there is a unique monic polynomial $p\\in\\mathcal{P}(F)$ of smallest degree such that $p(T)=0$. Furthermore, $\\deg(p) \\leq \\dim(V)$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0029",
      "sourceStart": 5973,
      "sourceEnd": 5975,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-2-3",
      "sourceStart": 5975,
      "sourceEnd": 6066,
      "original": "#### 2.2.3. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space and $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0030",
      "sourceStart": 6066,
      "sourceEnd": 6381,
      "sha256": "563df5352f2ff925b54b64d60c2e4fee96f2cf09068df5d21b2f7e7145b9f84e"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-2-4",
      "sourceStart": 6381,
      "sourceEnd": 6582,
      "original": "#### 2.2.4. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space, $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$, and $q\\in\\mathcal{P}(F)$. Then $q(T)=0$ if and only if $q$ is a polynomial multiple of the minimal polynomial of $T$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0031",
      "sourceStart": 6582,
      "sourceEnd": 6584,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-2-5",
      "sourceStart": 6584,
      "sourceEnd": 6846,
      "original": "#### 2.2.5. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space, $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$, $q\\in\\mathcal{P}(F)$, and $U$ is a subspace of $V$ that is invariant under $T$. Then the minimal polynomial of $T$ is a polynomial multiple of the minimal polynomial of $T|_{U}$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0032",
      "sourceStart": 6846,
      "sourceEnd": 6848,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-2-6",
      "sourceStart": 6848,
      "sourceEnd": 7036,
      "original": "#### 2.2.6. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space and $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$. Then $T$ is not invertible if and only if the constant term of the minimal polynomial of $T$ is zero.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0033",
      "sourceStart": 7036,
      "sourceEnd": 7119,
      "sha256": "5cd79b378b13901c07a80c03fa7a2f60cdc6a2a875f8b8ca431536700b272d79"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-2-7",
      "sourceStart": 7119,
      "sourceEnd": 7368,
      "original": "#### 2.2.7. Suppose $F=\\mathbb{C}$, $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$, $p\\in\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{C})$ is a nonconstant polynomial, and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$. Then $\\alpha$ is an eigenvalue of $p(T)$ if and only if $\\alpha=p(\\lambda)$ for some eigenvalue of $T$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0034",
      "sourceStart": 7368,
      "sourceEnd": 7370,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-2-8",
      "sourceStart": 7370,
      "sourceEnd": 7474,
      "original": "#### 2.2.8. Every operator on an odd-dimensional vector space has an eigenvalue, even if $F=\\mathbb{R}$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0035",
      "sourceStart": 7474,
      "sourceEnd": 7476,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-2-3",
      "sourceStart": 7476,
      "sourceEnd": 7510,
      "original": "### 2.3. Upper-Triangular Matrices",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0036",
      "sourceStart": 7510,
      "sourceEnd": 7512,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-3-1",
      "sourceStart": 7512,
      "sourceEnd": 7649,
      "original": "#### 2.3.1. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space with a basis $\\mathscr{B}=\\{v_1,\\ldots,v_n\\}$ and $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0037",
      "sourceStart": 7649,
      "sourceEnd": 8076,
      "sha256": "cbf94aefa4a5ec8ed1f563c962e182804e7f51e0421147ea29da541d4237fb40"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-3-2",
      "sourceStart": 8076,
      "sourceEnd": 8263,
      "original": "#### 2.3.2. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space with a basis with respect to which $T$ has an upper triangular matrix with diagonal entries $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_n$, then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0038",
      "sourceStart": 8263,
      "sourceEnd": 8737,
      "sha256": "c9a2a9528dc842a5caa3cfbb4ad5cc2c9b13582731d055957697990d850f4d47"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-3-3",
      "sourceStart": 8737,
      "sourceEnd": 9032,
      "original": "#### 2.3.3. Suppose $V$ is finite-dimensional and $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$. Then $T$ has an upper triangular matrix with respect to some basis of $V$ if and only if the minimal polynomial of $T$ equals $(z-\\lambda_1)\\cdots(z-\\lambda_m)$ for some $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m\\in F$, possibly repeated.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0039",
      "sourceStart": 9032,
      "sourceEnd": 9148,
      "sha256": "ecc04a93ecb821e5c39023e32fea5debb9a3cbf4c18b71a3b5c573e36d96c464"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-2-4",
      "sourceStart": 9148,
      "sourceEnd": 9181,
      "original": "### 2.4. Diagonalizable Operators",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0040",
      "sourceStart": 9181,
      "sourceEnd": 9183,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-4-1",
      "sourceStart": 9183,
      "sourceEnd": 9294,
      "original": "#### 2.4.1. Suppose $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$ and $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m$ are distinct eigenvalues of $T$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0041",
      "sourceStart": 9294,
      "sourceEnd": 9547,
      "sha256": "4e3dfbe5ab2370e822a67c8ca12e313df47cc60c1496958df3138cdfcfad9c99"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-4-2",
      "sourceStart": 9547,
      "sourceEnd": 9752,
      "original": "#### 2.4.2. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space and $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$. Let $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m$ denote the distinct eigenvalues of $T$. Then the following statements are equivalent.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0042",
      "sourceStart": 9752,
      "sourceEnd": 10063,
      "sha256": "3e23a0561f987c2fe65a5e93f39d7e8b44494642dadc40ee568c17f0e05982a0"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-4-3",
      "sourceStart": 10063,
      "sourceEnd": 10311,
      "original": "#### 2.4.3. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space and $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$. Then $T$ is diagonalizable if and only if the minimal polynomial of $T$ equals $(z-\\lambda_1)\\cdots(z-\\lambda_m)$ for distinct $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m\\in F$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0043",
      "sourceStart": 10311,
      "sourceEnd": 10374,
      "sha256": "ea44853df7d88edccabff3be622b6d97a3dcfb1572136e9b168e00c506cee391"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-4-4",
      "sourceStart": 10374,
      "sourceEnd": 10539,
      "original": "#### 2.4.4. Suppose $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$ is diagonalizable and $U$ is a subspace of $V$ that is invariant under $T$. Then $T|_U$ is a diagonalizable operator on $U$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0044",
      "sourceStart": 10539,
      "sourceEnd": 10541,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-4-5",
      "sourceStart": 10541,
      "sourceEnd": 10781,
      "original": "#### 2.4.5. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space, $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$ and $\\mathscr{B}$ is a basis of $V$. Then each eigenvalue of $T$ is contained in at least one Gershgorin disk of $T$ with respect to the basis $\\mathscr{B}$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0045",
      "sourceStart": 10781,
      "sourceEnd": 10783,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-2-5",
      "sourceStart": 10783,
      "sourceEnd": 10811,
      "original": "### 2.5. Commuting Operators",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0046",
      "sourceStart": 10811,
      "sourceEnd": 10813,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-5-1",
      "sourceStart": 10813,
      "sourceEnd": 11028,
      "original": "#### 2.5.1. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space, $S,T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$, and $\\mathscr{B}$ is a basis of $V$. Then $S$ and $T$ commute if and only if $[S]_{\\mathscr{B}}$ and $[T]_{\\mathscr{B}}$ commute.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0047",
      "sourceStart": 11028,
      "sourceEnd": 11030,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-5-2",
      "sourceStart": 11030,
      "sourceEnd": 11144,
      "original": "#### 2.5.2. Suppose $S,T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V)$ commute and $\\lambda\\in F$. Then $E(\\lambda,S)$ is invariant under $T$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0048",
      "sourceStart": 11144,
      "sourceEnd": 11146,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-5-3",
      "sourceStart": 11146,
      "sourceEnd": 11326,
      "original": "#### 2.5.3. Two diagonalizable operators on the same finite-dimensional vector space have diagonal matrices with respect to the same basis if and only if the two operators commute.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0049",
      "sourceStart": 11326,
      "sourceEnd": 11328,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-5-4",
      "sourceStart": 11328,
      "sourceEnd": 11452,
      "original": "#### 2.5.4. Every pair of commuting operators on a finite-dimensional nonzero complex vector space has a common eigenvector.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0050",
      "sourceStart": 11452,
      "sourceEnd": 11628,
      "sha256": "40124a89e0c243edd07f5b6adfa68ffcc5a63aaab6173b631a782d9391cb52e4"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-5-5",
      "sourceStart": 11628,
      "sourceEnd": 11831,
      "original": "#### 2.5.5. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional complex vector space and $S,T$ are commuting operators on $V$. Then there is a basis with respect to which both $S$ and $T$ have upper-triangular matrices.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0051",
      "sourceStart": 11831,
      "sourceEnd": 12076,
      "sha256": "9425ae89845810b12edb9169a7ef6afbbf60a2a1d4e115d6a4331b9bb04b600d"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-item-2-5-6",
      "sourceStart": 12076,
      "sourceEnd": 12191,
      "original": "#### 2.5.6. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional complex vector space and $S,T$ are commuting operators on $V$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch05-block-0052",
      "sourceStart": 12191,
      "sourceEnd": 12357,
      "sha256": "73e5c0098cdaa140f98c04ded0ed6b3241dcb1c100ccdc839da24bdd1b4a8845"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch05-section-3",
      "sourceStart": 12357,
      "sourceEnd": 12371,
      "original": "## 3. Examples",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
